      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 The Authors. Clinical & Experimental Allergy Published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "NFATc1 isoforms are highly regulated in peripheral T cells where they contribute to the effector function and cell homeostasis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Here, we investigated the role of NFATc1 in asthma and in T cells."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In a murine model of allergic asthma, we analysed differences in T-cell development in this allergic disease model, between wild-type and NFATc1 conditional knockout mice. Thus, we performed quantitative real-time PCR to investigate the mRNA expression of Th2-associated genes as well as genes that are involved in IgE immunoglobulin class-switch. Additionally, we used ELISA, Western blot and flow cytometry (FACS) to analyse protein concentrations of Th1-, Th2- and Th17-specific transcription factors and cytokines and the Th2 chemokine, thymus and activation-regulated chemokine/chemokine ligand 17 (TARC/CCL17) by ELISA."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Mice lacking NFATc1 in CD4(+) T cells display a significant reduction in lung Th2 and Th17 as well as an increase of Th1 cells in an experimental asthma model. Additionally, Batf gene, a recently described transcription factor of the Th2 and Th17 cell differentiation as well as a key T and B transcription factor involved in the IgE immunoglobulin class-switch, was found decreased in the lungs of these mice. As a consequence, serum OVA-specific IgE and IgG1 levels were found significantly decreased after allergen exposure and in the absence of NFATc1 in T cells in experimental allergic asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS AND CLINICAL RELEVANCE",
            "content": "Targeting NFATc1 in T lymphocytes ameliorated the allergic trait in the airways of NFATc1(fl/fl) xCD4Cre mice. NFATc1 emerges as a novel target for anti-allergy intervention."
          }
        ]
      },
